  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The Heat Wave will peak with Heat Advisories likely \nfor a good portion of the area from Wednesday into Friday.\n\nThe high heat and humidity will really get going this afternoon when \nhighs will top out in the 90s. Heat indices are expected to fall \njust short of the Heat Advisory criteria of 105 degrees for several \nhours. Most areas from the Triangle east and south will have heat \nindices of 100 to 104, thus it would still be a good idea to prepare \nfor the day as if an advisory is in effect.\n\nThe heat will peak this week from Wednesday into Friday. This is \nwhen the upper ridge will park overhead at 595dm overhead of the TN \nvalley to the mid-Atlantic coast. This is slightly lower heights \nthan what was observed during the early July heat wave. Other than \nan isolated thunderstorm, chances less than 15 percent at any one \npoint, expect mostly sunny hot days and very warm and humid nights \nfrom tonight into Friday. Highs should be hottest Wednesday and \nThursday (mid 90s to near 100), with Friday potentially as hot. \n\nEven overnight lows will be in the mid to upper 70s for many areas. \nAn Excessive Heat Watch was considered for the Triangle and Coastal \nPlain for Wed-Fri (as overnight lows will struggle to fall below 80 \nand heat indices potentially approach 110 during the peak of the \nafternoon heat each day). We held off for now - but it will be \nconsidered in later shifts. \n\nHeat index values in the upper 90s to around 105 degrees are \nexpected each day with areas from Raleigh east and south will be \nmost likely to surpass 105 degrees. Some areas may approach 110 \ndegrees during peak heating. Heat advisories appear likely. \n\nThere is a chance of mainly PM thunderstorms Friday and Saturday as \nthe upper ridge begins to break down and shift back to the west for \na time. Temperatures will back off some, especially Saturday and \nSunday when mostly mid to upper 80s are expected. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Labor Day weekend through Monday gradually trends \ncooler along with highest thunderstorm chances on Saturday. \n\nEnsemble guidance continues to show a gradual westward shift of the \nupper ridge this weekend, allowing troughing and an approaching cold \nfront to bring a cooler pattern change to central NC by later in the \nweekend. Temps still expected to trend slightly downward Saturday, \nbut still remaining well above normal with highs in the lower to mid \n90s.  A more substantial cooldown is expected Sunday into Labor Day \nwith highs in the mid 80s. High pressure building in behind the \nfront may also bring a reduction in humidity by Monday.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances should increase Friday into Saturday \nas the front approaches, with the best opportunity for convection \nSaturday. Scattered showers or storms could potentially linger into \nSunday, but the best chances will be particularly south and east of \nRaleigh. Overall, the trend toward cooler and drier conditions for \nSunday and Labor Day is becoming increasingly well supported. While \na severe threat cannot be ruled out Saturday, predictability remains \ntoo low to determine the magnitude or coverage of any severe weather \nthreat at this time.",
